    Implementation Notes for the Game Logic
    To get this running on a 6x6 board with 18 matches, you should handle the setup in your main game class (e.g., Board.java or Game.java) like this:

    Generate IDs: Create a list containing two of every integer from 0 to 17.

    Shuffle: Use Collections.shuffle() to randomize the IDs.

    Instantiate: Loop through your 6x6 grid and create a new PolygonTile(pos, id).

    Logic Breakdown
    The Offset: By setting numSides = kindID + 3, an ID of 0 becomes a triangle, and an ID of 17 becomes a 20-sided polygon.

    Visual Clarity: As the number of sides increases (like 18 vs 19 vs 20), the polygons start looking very much like circles. I set the radius to 40% of the tile size and added a BasicStroke(3) to help the player distinguish the edges more easily.

    Neutrality: The call to setBackground(Color.LIGHT_GRAY) in the constructor is crucial—it "breaks" the P3Tools.getSwatchColor logic from the parent class so the player is forced to look at the shape to find a match.